WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.692
When you touch an object, each fingertip meets a small part of its surface.

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:12.282
That local surface has a direction. A small contact plate can turn to match it.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:16.528
The Fresnel series explores how this simple contact-plane

00:00:16.528 --> 00:00:20.912
tilt can suggest shape, stiffness, weight distribution, and inertia.

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:29.115
Here is the original CAD geometry from the World Haptics twenty twenty-five demo design.

00:00:29.465 --> 00:00:37.025
Three moving surfaces meet the thumb, index finger, and middle finger. Grip force provides an input.

00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:41.028
Watch the contact surfaces turn around their own pivots.

00:00:41.028 --> 00:00:46.711
The virtual object beside them shows what that changing touch is meant to represent.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:54.147
First, shape. Imagine grasping different parts of a bottle.

00:00:54.497 --> 00:01:00.257
The side is almost straight. Near the shoulder, the local surface leans.

00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:04.939
The contact plates change orientation to follow those local directions.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:09.271
The fingertip cues can agree with the object you see.

00:01:09.621 --> 00:01:16.749
The mechanism does not build the whole bottle. It presents a few carefully chosen contact surfaces.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.948
Next, squeeze. Grip force drives indentation of the virtual surface.

00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:33.650
A softer region deforms more for the same input. As its shape changes, the local contact directions change too.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.672
In the twenty twenty-five deformation study, the index and

00:01:37.672 --> 00:01:41.752
middle contact planes moved while the thumb plane stayed fixed.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:46.491
The study found better agreement between what people saw and felt.

00:01:46.491 --> 00:01:51.678
The change in softness ratings was a trend, not a statistically clear result.

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:59.449
Now tilt the bottle. The red dot marks its virtual center of gravity.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:06.375
When the mass is offset from the grasp, gravity creates a turning effect that changes with the bottle's angle.

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:11.114
The proposed rendering model changes the fingertip-plane cue accordingly.

00:02:11.114 --> 00:02:17.453
This suggests a different weight distribution, without moving real ballast inside the mechanism.

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:25.057
Finally, move the bottle back and forth. Then move it up and down.

00:02:25.407 --> 00:02:31.767
The cue changes with acceleration: how the motion speeds up, slows down, or reverses.

00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:36.590
The published model treats horizontal and vertical motion separately,

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.565
using coordinated fingertip-plane tilt to suggest inertial effects.

00:02:40.915 --> 00:02:44.937
The arrows and moving surfaces here explain that relationship.

00:02:44.937 --> 00:02:50.299
They are illustrative commands, not a recording of measured forces or motion.

00:02:52.467 --> 00:03:00.147
Across the Fresnel series, the contact principle stays the same. The input and the rendering rule change.

00:03:00.497 --> 00:03:09.065
Grasp for shape. Squeeze for stiffness. Tilt for weight distribution. Shake for inertia.

00:03:09.415 --> 00:03:15.559
The virtual object, the finger contact, and the mechanical movement should tell the same story.

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:23.709
This film draws on the original Fresnel research and the twenty twenty-five bottle demonstration.

00:03:24.059 --> 00:03:30.330
One CAD model illustrates the series here. Earlier prototypes and experiments differ.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:35.555
The transcript links the primary sources and explains the limits.

